<title>BELGIUM: EU sees veterinary pact main issue in U.S. talks.</title>
<headline>EU sees veterinary pact main issue in U.S. talks.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-11-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>A veterinary equivalency standards agreement is expected to be the main issue in talks next week with U.S. officials, a Commission spokesman said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>U.S. Agriculture Secretary Dan Glickman's trade adviser Paul Drazek will head the team during the talks, which will include grain export subsidies and rice quotas, with the European Commission in Brussels next Tuesday.</p>
<p>&quot;There are still problems,&quot; the spokesman said, noting disagreements over pet food, dairy products and eggs.</p>
<p>&quot;It's difficult to see them solved by January 1, 1997 (when the EU introduces common import rules,&quot; the spokesman added.</p>
<p>The introduction of common rules, which for some EU member states means higher standards, follows the completion of a single EU market in January 1993.</p>
<p>The deadline has been postponed several times and EU member states opposed any further delay.</p>
<p>&quot;The EU will not block imports but it means more veterinary inspections,&quot; the spokesman said.</p>
<p>Over $500 million of U.S. meat exports would be affected.</p>
</text>
